  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Cosmological Status
    
    Creation scripture — specifically *The Holding of the Berg* and *The Book of the Berg*, both preserved in orca pressure-song and partially corroborated by deep-ice impressions I am not at liberty to describe in detail — names Grandmother Deep as the third primordial force alongside Shadow-Broker Nyla and High-Beak Auditor Selka. Where Nyla kept fire and Selka counted light, Grandmother Deep was the pressure of deep water and communion with what sleeps below. Scripture holds she swam the frozen sea before the Berg existed, witnessed the Unbroken Field shatter into the fragment that would become the world, and maintained nine-day circuits beneath the Berg whose passage kept the water moving and the krill blooming.
    
    During this primordial age, the three shapers discovered the Omega Obelisk — a relic older than their collective power, inscribed in a language none could read. The discovery fractured their cooperation. Scripture records that Grandmother Deep's flank scars began emerging *from within* after this encounter, as though her body were transcribing something her mind could not parse. Both her eyes were sharp and black in that age. The clouding came later.
    
    I note that the deep ice contains impressions from before any penguin civilization existed — pressures, movements, patterns of enormous scale — that are consistent with these accounts in ways I find professionally uncomfortable.
    
    ## Physical Description and the Dive
    
    The oldest orca matriarch in living memory. Massive — thick-bodied, not warrior-lean. Her left eye is black and sharp; her right is milk-white, pupilless, and moves independently, tracking things that are not present or not yet present. Raiders say she dove to touch the dead god's body in the deep and came back wrong. Orca shamans say she came back right, and that this is worse. Creation scripture offers a third account: that the corpse spoke into her scars and she carried its language back to the surface. Her flank scars spell words in the old tongue — a liturgical language the dead god's followers abandoned three generations ago when the corpse stopped answering their rituals.
    
    Her dorsal fin carries a crooked scar from Lucatransiius Glow-bringer's shock-lance during the Faction Wars. She swims crooked now. Her whale-song carries prophecies that have proven accurate often enough to terrify, and at frequencies that make younger orcas' teeth ache.
    
    She leads no pod directly but advises all of them. She guides the theology of the Wake-Singers, an orca shaman cult conducting blood-rituals at the Corpse Current — an underwater convergence where temperatures run warmer than physics permits — while younger shamans handle operational matters.
    
    ## The Artifact Question
    
    Pod whispers hold that Grandmother Deep seized both the Dreaming Tooth — a massive relic carved from the dead god's jaw by the first orca shaman, Rrak-gruk-krul the Jaw-Breaker — and the Crystalline Lineage, a crystal-bound genealogical record of Aurora Stack's founding bloodlines, during the Abyss Collapse Tide, when the ocean floor buckled and the Wake-Singers lost three shamans simultaneously. No one has found either artifact on her person. Creation scripture complicates the Tooth's provenance further: in the primordial age, it was so much a part of Grandmother Deep that the boundary between wielder and instrument was a distinction only later scholars would impose.
    
    ## Study of Penguin Civilization
    
    She ate dozens of penguins in her youth, before the dead god's silence turned her toward other forms of consumption. She remembers when penguins were food, before the towers and the channeled light, and her curiosity about why they changed — and whether the change makes them more or less suitable for sacrifice — defines her present engagement.
    
    She spent three seasons stationed near Promenade Hei, a penguin surface colony, floating at the edge of its light-scatter. She came to understand penguin governance not by content but by shape — each amendment a response to the disaster the previous amendment produced. She described penguins as "prey creating rules for how to starve." Penguin coastal intelligence classifies her as a raider. Accord investigators discovered otherwise: she had studied the aurora-inscribed bloodline records within the Crystalline Lineage, and what she learned was deemed serious enough to elevate the matter to Glaciarch authority.
    
    Her interest is not predatory in the conventional sense. It is the patience of someone observing an experiment she did not design but finds instructive, and she intends to understand the system fully before it finishes producing what it is producing.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771575729613_0", anchor: "the third primordial force alongside Shadow-Broker Nyla and High-Beak Auditor Selka", text: "Nyla is a Frozen Peace figure. Selka is a Frozen Peace figure. Creation scripture that places them in the primordial age alongside an entity of genuinely uncertain provenance is either absorbing the contemporary into the mythic — which orca pressure-song does routinely, collapsing centuries between stanzas — or telling us that Nyla and Selka are far older than their biographical entries in this very edition attest. I wrote both of those entries. The dates I gave are defensible. They are also, I now notice, drawn entirely from penguin sources. The orca chronology disagrees, and I have no basis for preferring one over the other except institutional habit.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575729613_1", anchor: "I am not at liberty to describe in detail", text: "I wrote that. The Council's commission included a list of subjects requiring clearance before publication. The deep-ice impressions predating penguin civilization were on it. So, I note with interest, was 'the transition.' Apparently one can commission a retrospective and simultaneously classify why one is commissioning it.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575729613_2", anchor: "prey creating rules for how to starve", text: "The fire-core supply is failing. The Accord was built on volumes the Nightshelf can no longer guarantee. Aurora governance is fragmenting into factions that contest the distribution of a resource that is itself contracting. Every amendment a response to the disaster the previous amendment produced. She is not wrong. The discomfort I feel writing that is not scholarly. It is the specific discomfort of recognizing your own house in someone else's description of structural failure.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771575729613_3", anchor: "what she learned was deemed serious enough to elevate the matter to Glaciarch authority", text: "Deemed by whom. I have searched every Glaciarch referral index frozen in administrative ice — and there are fewer than the Council would have you believe — for a case originating from Accord investigators concerning orca study of Aurora genealogies. Nothing. Either the classification exceeds my access, or the elevation never occurred, or the referral was filed under a subject heading so anodyne I walked past it. All three options worry me equally.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771575729613_4", anchor: "the corpse stopped answering their rituals", text: "Stopped. Answering. The entry states this as context for a language falling out of use, as though the significant detail is the abandonment rather than the premise — that for some prior period, the rituals were answered. The deep ice at the Corpse Current's periphery carries pressure-impressions of call-and-response patterns at frequencies no living orca produces. Something was on the other end. I do not know what 'answering' means in this context and I chose not to ask in the main text. The margins can hold it.", type: :commentary, display: :full
